## ABSTRACT
Efficient and selective extraction of uranium (U(VI)) from seawater is essential for sustainable nuclear power production. This study reports a novel adsorbent zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF)-67@SiO<sub>2</sub>-A/polyacrylamide (PAM) which was synthesized by grafting the core–shell metal–organic frameworks (MOFs)-based nanostructures coated with the 3-aminopropyl triethoxysilane (APTES) functionalized SiO<sub>2</sub> (SiO<sub>2</sub>-A) onto PAM hydrogel. The SiO<sub>2</sub> shell was grown on the surface of MOF, which improved the acid-base resistance of MOF. The introduction of ZIF-67@SiO<sub>2</sub>-A enhances the specific surface area and adsorption efficiency of the PAM. The ZIF-67@SiO<sub>2</sub>-A/PAM shows remarkable adsorption capacity, fast adsorption kinetics, and good reusability for uranium. It has excellent adsorption property (6.33 mg·g<sup>-1</sup>, 30 d) in natural seawater. The X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), Fourier transform infrared (FTIR), energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) mappings, and density functional theory reveal that the coordination by N and O in ZIF-67@SiO<sub>2</sub>-A/PAM with uranium is the main mechanism of uranium adsorption. Thus, ZIF-67@SiO<sub>2</sub>-A/PAM has great potential to capture uranium from natural seawater.
## KEYWORDS
hydrogel, metal–organic frameworks, core–shelled nanostructures, uranium extraction from seawater
## 1 Introduction
As the main clean energy, nuclear energy is expected to replace fossil fuel energy due to its advantages of saving energy and not emitting greenhouse gases [1–4]. Uranium (U) is an important raw material for nuclear power generation, but terrestrial uranium resources are limited and it is strenuous to meet the growing nuclear industry's demand for uranium [5–8]. Notably, there are huge reserves of uranium in seawater, around 4.5 billion tons in total. However, the extremely low concentration of uranium in seawater and the presence of many coexisting ions make the absorption of uranium in seawater a great challenge [9–12]. Therefore, it is vital to design adsorbents that could high effectively extract uranium from seawater.
In recent years, polyacrylamide (PAM) based hydrogels have garnered immense attention owing to their abundant hydrophilic functional groups and good adsorption capacity [13, 14]. They are used as adsorbents to achieve efficient uranium extraction. Xu et al. prepared carboxymethyl cellulose-PAM/Mg(OH)<sub>2</sub> (CMCPAM/Mg(OH)<sub>2</sub>) adsorbents for uranium adsorption. The adsorption capacities of uranium at pH = 5 and pH = 8 were 1584.67 and 454.55 mg·g<sup>-1</sup>, respectively [15]. Wang et al. reported a stable montmorillonite-polydopamine/PAM (MMTPDA/PAM) nanocomposite hydrogel for capturing uranium from water. The adsorption capacity of MMT-PDA/PAM for uranium in algae-contained simulated seawater can reach 2130 μg·g<sup>-1</sup> [16].
However, pure PAM adsorbent contains fewer functional groups and has a low specific surface area, so its adsorption effect on uranium is limited. Therefore, it is urgent to seek polyacrylamide based materials with high adsorption capacity and large specific surface area.
Metal–organic framework (MOF) is a porous coordination polymer formed by the self-assembly of metal ions with organic ligands [17, 18]. MOFs are widely considered as promising candidate materials for uranium adsorption due to their controllable pore size, excellent chemical stability, and abundant binding sites [8, 19–21]. Su et al. investigated zeolitic imidazole framework (ZIF)-67 for the elimination of U(VI) from simulated seawater. It achieved the high extraction rate of uranium (approx. 95%) in the concentration range of 3–500 μg·L<sup>-1</sup> [22]. Wu et al. synthesized rod-like MOF (MOF-5) nanomaterials for adsorption of U(VI) from aqueous solution. The maximum U(VI) adsorption quantity of MOF-5 was 237.0 mg·g<sup>-1</sup> at pH 5.0 [23]. However, the adsorption quantity of U(VI) by traditional single MOF materials is low, and the cost is high. It is found that the core–shell nanocomposites based on MOF have a potential application prospect in uranium extraction attributable to their high porosity and large specific surface area. With MOF as the core and SiO<sub>2</sub> inorganic materials with high specific surface area and chemical stability as shells, the structure is expected to extract uranium efficiently from seawater [24, 25].
Herein, a novel adsorbent ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM was prepared by cross-linking surface-functionalized ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\) (ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A) of core–shelled structure with PAM hydrogel for the extraction of U(VI) in seawater. The surface of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\) nanoparticles was functionalized with 3-aminopropyl triethoxysilane (APTES), followed by grafting of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A into the PAM polymer network by the addition of glutaraldehyde (GA). The introduction of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A core–shelled structure not only increases the specific surface area and structural stability, but also presents the high adsorption capacity. The structure and morphology of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM were determined by X-ray diffraction (XRD), Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy, scanning electron microscopy (SEM), and transmission electron microscopy (TEM). The adsorption behavior of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM under different conditions was studied systematically, and the potential adsorption mechanism of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM for U(VI) was discussed in detail via X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), FTIR, energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) mappings, and theoretical density functional theory (DFT) calculations. At last, the adsorption properties of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM adsorbent in natural seawater were also studied.
