## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Material characterization
3.1 Material characterizationZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM was prepared by chemical cross-linking ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A and PAM (Figs. 1(a) and 1(b)). Firstly, ZIF-67 nanocrystals were pre-treated with the methanol/deionized water mixture solution. The core–shelled ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\) nanoparticles were synthesized by growing SiO\(_2\) shells and etching ZIF-67 core simultaneously in an alkaline environment. The ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\) was then surface functionalized with APTES. Secondly, ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A was grafted to PAM network by GA crosslinking to obtain ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM. In particular, GA was used as a crosslinking agent to connect PAM and SiO\(_2\)-A by nucleophilic addition reaction between aldehyde group and amino group, and the feasibility of the principle was demonstrated by DFT simulation calculation. Electrostatic potential (ESP) was used to describe the possible active sites of the reaction. As shown in Figs. 1(c)–1(f), \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) on PAM and SiO\(_2\)-A has obvious positive potential, while the two aldehyde groups of GA have obvious negative potential, indicating that the principle of GA crosslinking agent is feasible [26, 27].The FTIR and XRD were used to verify the successful synthesis of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM. In Fig. 2(a), the wide peaks at 3432 and 3200 cm\(^{-1}\) are attributed to strong and symmetrical tensile vibrations of the N–H bond in PAM [28]. For ZIF-67, the peaks at
The FTIR and XRD were used to verify the successful synthesis of ZIF-67@SiO2-A/PAM. In Fig. 2(a), the wide peaks at 3432 and \(3200~\ c m^{-1}\) are attributed to strong and symmetrical tensile vibrations of the N–H bond in PAM [28]. For ZIF-67, the peaks at
1585 and 755 cm\(^{-1}\) belong to the stretching vibrations of C=N and C–N on the imidazole ring [29, 30]. For the spectrum of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A, the stretching vibrations of Si–O–Si appear at 1060 cm\(^{-1}\) and the peaks at 2961 and 2868 cm\(^{-1}\) are caused by the symmetric and antisymmetric stretching vibrations of methylidene of APTES. The stretching vibrations of C=N and C–N on the imidazole ring appear at 1585 and 755 cm\(^{-1}\). The appearance of these peaks proves that APTES successfully modified ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\) [31, 32]. This result was further proved by XPS analysis (Fig. 2(b) and Table S1 in the ESM). In this respect, the N 1s peak intensity of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A is increased compared with that of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\), indicating that the functionalized ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\) nanoparticles are coated with a layer of APTES. After ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A is fixed to the PAM network, the stretching vibrations of C=N and C–N occur at 1585 and 755 cm\(^{-1}\). The appearance of peaks at 1060, 3432, and 3200 cm\(^{-1}\) indicates that the synthesis of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM was successful [33–35]. In Fig. 2(c), the XRD spectra of ZIF-67 exhibit that the diffraction peaks of 7.5°, 10.5°, 12.8°, and 18.2° correspond to the (110), (200), (211), and (222) planes, respectively [36, 37]. For ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A, the appearance of the characteristic peak of ZIF-67 and the characteristic signal of SiO\(_2\) at 2θ of 18–27° prove the successful synthesis of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A [38]. The wide absorption band at 22.2° belongs to the regular carbon chain of PAM [39]. After the introduction of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A into PAM network, the XRD spectra of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM also appeared in the above typical diffraction peaks, implying that ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A was successfully grafted into PAM network. The N\(_2\) adsorption–desorption isotherms of PAM, ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A, and ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM are shown in Figs. 2(d)–2(f). The curves of PAM, ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A, and ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM are type-IV, implying that they have mesoporous structures [40]. The surface areas of PAM, ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A, and ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM are 40.52, 1055.77, and 600.49 m\(^2\)-g\(^{-1}\), respectively, indicating that the introduction of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A increased the specific surface area of the PAM. The average pore sizes (inset) of PAM, ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A and ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM are 9.2 ± 0.16, 6.68 ± 0.32, and 3.67 ± 0.84 nm, respectively.The microscopic morphology of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A was observed by SEM, and the results are shown in Fig. 3. It can be seen from
The microscopic morphology of ZIF-67@SiO2-A was observed by SEM, and the results are shown in Fig. 3. It can be seen from
Figs. 3(a) and 3(b) that the shape and size of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A nanoparticles are uniform and maintain the rhomboidal dodecahedron structure. TEM images (Figs. 3(c) and 3(d)) confirmed that the ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A was a core–shelled structure. In the EDS mappings image of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A, the uniform distribution of C, N, O, Co, and Si was observed (Fig. 3(e)). These results suggested the successful construction of the core–shell structure of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A. The morphologies of PAM and ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM were also characterized by SEM. In Figs. 3(f) and 3(g), PAM has a regular three-dimensional network structure. Figure 3(h) shows that ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM preserves the three-dimensional network structure, proving that the incorporation of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A does not destroy the network structure of PAM. The SEM image (Fig. 3(j)) of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM shows that ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A is distributed on the PAM network. Meanwhile, the EDS image element distribution map (Fig. 3(j)) shows that C, N, O, Si, and Co are uniformly dispersed in ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM, further indicating the successful introduction of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A.
## 3.2 Adsorption properties
The effect of different pH values on the adsorption performance of uranium by PAM and ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM was investigated by adjusting the pH in the range of 3–10. In Fig. 4(a), the adsorption capacity of PAM for uranium increases with the increase of pH value. Compared with PAM, the uranium adsorption quantity of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM increased significantly after the introduction of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A. This is due to the increased active sites and specific surface area of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A. ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM reaches the maximum adsorption capacity (173.48 mg·g\(^{-1}\)) at pH = 6.0, \(C_0 = 99.73 \, \text{mg·L}^{-1}\). For the reason that at low pH, \(-\text{NH}_2\) protonates to form \(-\text{NH}_3\), which has electrostatic repulsion with \(\text{UO}_2^{2+}\) in solution, resulting in low adsorption capacity. As the pH rises to 6, \(-\text{NH}_3^+\) deprotonation produces \(-\text{NH}_2\), which increases the adsorption points for
adsorption uranium [41, 42]. As the pH value increases from 6 to 10, the adsorption of uranium by ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM decreases, which is associated with the combination of uranyl ions with carbonate groups to form \([\text{UO}_2(\text{CO}_3)_3]^+\) in alkaline solution [43]. Therefore, the addition of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A is an effective strategy for improving the extraction capacity of PAM-based adsorbents for U(VI).
The adsorption kinetics of PAM and ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM were studied through a range of adsorption tests with different reaction times. In Fig. 4(b), the adsorption of U(VI) by the two adsorbents was rapid in the initial stage and gradually reached equilibrium. As can be seen in the corresponding enlarged diagram, adsorption uranium by PAM reached equilibrium at 300 min. In contrast to PAM, the adsorption time of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM reached equilibrium at 180 min. The adsorption quantity of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM increased and the equilibrium time shortened, indicating that the incorporation of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A improved the adsorption rate of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM on U(VI). Besides, the desorption efficiency of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM with different uranium adsorption times in reaction kinetics was tested. The results showed that the uranium adsorption time did not greatly affect the desorption efficiency of the adsorbent, and the desorption rate could reach 95.3% at 600 min (Fig. S1 in the ESM). In addition, the desorption efficiency of uranium by the adsorbent no longer changed at 180 min, and the desorption reached equilibrium. The decrease of uranium desorption efficiency was attributed to the accumulation of tightly bound uranium occupying functional groups on the adsorbent, resulting in a reduction of the adsorbent active sites [13, 44]. In order to analyze the behavior of adsorption kinetics, the experimental data were fitted by pseudo-second-order (Fig. 4(c)) and pseudo-first-order (Fig. S2 in the ESM) models [45, 46]. The results present that the pseudo-second-order model of PAM and ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM was better than pseudo-first-order, and the correlation coefficients (\(R^2\)) (Table S2 in the ESM) are 0.9941 and 0.9999, respectively, indicating that chemical adsorption is the main
control step during the uranium extraction process of the two adsorbents. The consequences of the Morris–Weber (M–W) model fitting analysis showed that the adsorption process of PAM and ZIF-67@SiO \(2\) -A/PAM consisted of two stages (Fig. 4(d)) [13]. Firstly, U(VI) reaches the adsorbent surface for surface capture, where the surface functional groups of the adsorbent play a major role. Secondly, with the increase of adsorption time, U(VI) diffused into the adsorbent and further adsorbed.
The uranium capture isotherms of PAM and ZIF-67@SiO \(2\) - A/PAM at different initial uranium concentrations were determined. In Fig. 4(e), the extraction quantity of U(VI) by PAM and ZIF-67@SiO \(2\) -A/PAM increases with the increase of initial uranium concentration and gradually reaches equilibrium. The equilibrium adsorption capacities of PAM and ZIF-67@SiO \(2\) - A/PAM were 254.21 and \(450.36~\mathrm{mg{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) \(\mathrm{pH}=6.0\) , \(T=25\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) \(C_{0}=\) \(398.25~\mathrm{mg{\cdot}L^{-1}})\) , respectively. The results displayed that the uptake capacity of ZIF-67@SiO \(2\) -A/PAM for U(VI) was increased by the addition of ZIF-67@SiO \(2\) -A. The adsorption properties of ZIF\(67@S i O_{2}\) -A/PAM for uranium were compared with those conventional adsorbents (Table S5 in the ESM). It was found that the ZIF-67@SiO \(2\) -A/PAM has excellent uranium adsorption properties at \(25\,^{\circ}\!C.\) Langmuir model and Freundlich model were used for data fitting analysis (Fig. 4(f), Fig. S3, and Table S3 in the ESM) [47–49]. The results exhibit that Langmuir model was better than Freundlich model at different temperatures, indicating that the single-layer adsorption is the main process of uranium adsorption. Moreover, the adsorption capacity achieved exceeds that of some reported adsorbents (Table S4 in the ESM), such as polyacrylonitrile nanofibrous membrane, polyacrylamide-based composite hydrogel, and amidoximated silver-silica core–shell nanoparticles. These results indicate that ZIF-67@SiO \(2\) -A/PAM has excellent potential for uranium capture.
## 3.3 Selectivity and reusability
Since seawater contains many competitive ions, adsorption selectivity is an important factor affecting the application of adsorbent [50, 51]. The adsorption selectivity of ZIF-67@SiO \(2\) -
Since seawater contains many competitive ions, adsorption selectivity is an important factor affecting the application of adsorbent [50, 51]. The adsorption selectivity of ZIF-67@SiO \(2\) - A/PAM for uranyl ions in solution containing uranium and 10 other metal ions was evaluated. Figure 5(a) and Table S6 in the ESM show that ZIF-67@SiO \(2\) -A/PAM displayed excellent uranium adsorption selectivity when co-existing with various metal ions, and the removal rate of uranyl ions reached \(95\%\) . In addition, \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) and V have a great impact on the capture of U(VI) in the experiment, because \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) easily forms hydroxide and adheres to the surface of ZIF-67@SiO \(2\) -A/PAM, while \(\mathrm{V}\) is the species closest to U(VI) in charge and size and is considered to be the main competitor in the tests of U(VI) capture [13]. At the same time, the distribution coefficient \((K_{\mathrm{d}})\) is \(53.466~\mathrm{\mL{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) , indicating the strong binding of ZIF-67@SiO \(2\) -A/PAM with uranium (Fig. 5(b)). The uranium adsorption properties of adsorbents in the presence of various anions were also investigated. From Fig. S4 in the ESM, it can be seen that the removal of uranium by the adsorbent was low in the presence of \(\mathrm{CO}_{3}^{\ 2-}\) and \(\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{\ 2-}\) , which may be due to the complexation of carbonate with uranium to form uranyl carbonate. Similarly, sulfate formed complexes with uranium at low \(\mathrm{pH}\) , decreasing the adsorption capacity of ZIF-67@SiO \(2\) -A/PAM [52]. Moreover, the uranium extraction capacity of ZIF-67@SiO \(2\) -A/PAM adsorbent in natural seawater (Yellow Sea in Qingdao, Shandong Province, China) has been measured. In Fig. 5(c), after 30 days of adsorption test, the extraction amount of uranium by ZIF-67@SiO \(2\) -A/PAM reached \(6.33~\mathrm{\mg{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) . Compared with the reported adsorption materials, ZIF-67@SiO \(2\) -A/PAM has excellent adsorption properties (Table S7 in the ESM). Furthermore, Table S8 in the ESM shows the concentration of competing ions present in natural seawater. ZIF-67@SiO \(2\) -A/PAM presents a good selectivity for U(VI) in natural seawater where multiple metal ions coexist (Fig. 5(d)).
Besides, the reusability of the ZIF-67@SiO \(2\) -A/PAM is essential for the extraction of U(VI) from seawater [53]. The elution efficiency of various reagents as desorption agents was explored, and NaOH, which exhibited the highest elution efficiency, was selected as the desorption agent (Table S9 in the ESM). In Fig. S5(a) in the ESM, the uranium elution rate decreases as the cycle time increases. This is due to the decrease of the mass of the
adsorbent and the reduction of the number of active sites [28]. But after the \(10^{\mathrm{th}}\) cycle, the elution rate still reached \((82.45\,\pm\,2.76)\%\) . The adsorption efficiency of the adsorbent maintained at \(87.76\%\) over 10 cycles of adsorption experiments (Fig. S6 in the ESM). The higher adsorption efficiency and elution rate reveal that ZIF\(67@S i O_{2}\) -A/PAM has good reusability. At the same time, FTIR spectra after each cycle (Fig. S5(b) in the ESM) further confirmed the structural stability of ZIF-67@SiO2-A/PAM adsorbent. The ZIF-67@SiO2-A/PAM samples were immersed in aqueous solutions with \(\mathrm{pH}\) value of 3–10 for \(^{5\,\mathrm{h},}\) and analyzed their FTIR and XRD spectra (Fig. S7 in the ESM). Peaks located at 1585, 755, 1060, 3432, and \(3200~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}\) were still observed at different pHs, which attributed to the stretching vibration peaks of \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N},\) , C–N, and Si–O–Si stretching vibrations, and tensile vibrations of N–H bond in PAM, respectively. Moreover, in the corresponding XRD plots, the peaks located at \(7.5^{\circ}\) , \(10.5^{\circ}\) , \(12.8^{\circ}\) , and \(18.2^{\circ}\) correspond to the diffraction peaks of ZIF-67, and the broad absorption band at \(22.2^{\circ}\) attributed to the conventional carbon chains of PAM. The presence of these peaks indicated that the structure of the adsorbent was not disrupted at different pH. All these experiments' results indicate that ZIF-67@SiO2-A/PAM has good acid and alkali resistance stability.
## 3.4 Adsorption mechanism
The interaction mechanism between uranium and ZIF-67@SiO2- A/PAM was characterized by XPS, FTIR, and EDS mappings. As shown in Fig. 6(a), the XPS spectra of ZIF-67@SiO2-A/PAM-U exhibited obvious U 4f double peaks, which proved the successful adsorption of uranium by the adsorbent [54]. In Fig. 6(b), the highresolution XPS spectrum of O 1s before adsorption exhibited four distinguishable peaks located at 531.44, 530.64, 532.85, and \(531.21~\mathrm{eV},\) , which were assigned to O–H, \(\scriptstyle\mathrm{O=C}\) , \(-\mathrm{O}-\) , and \(\mathrm{Co-O},\) , respectively. After U(VI) loading (Fig. 6(c)), the peaks of O–H and \(\mathrm{Co-O}\) move to 531.92 and \(531.41\;\mathrm{eV},\) , respectively, and a new peak of \(\mathrm{O}{=}\mathrm{U}{=}\mathrm{O}\) occurs at \(530.94\;\mathrm{eV}.\) . The results show that the oxygen in ZIF-67@SiO2-A/PAM has a coordinating effect with uranium [55, 56]. In Fig. 6(d), the N 1s spectrum of ZIF-67@SiO2-A/PAM is fitted by peaks at 399.41, 400.45, and \(398.82~\mathrm{eV},\) , corresponding to \(\mathrm{=N-}\) , –NH–, and –NH2, respectively. After uranium adsorption
(Fig. 6(e)), the peaks of \(\mathrm{=N-}\) and \(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) moved to 399.27 and \(398.75\;\mathrm{eV},\) while the peaks of \(\mathrm{-NH-}\) did not change significantly. This indicates that the amino group in ZIF-67@SiO2-A/PAM is involved in the adsorption process of uranium [57]. It can be seen from the high-resolution XPS spectrum of C 1s (Fig. S8 in the ESM) that after uranium adsorption, the binding energy of \(\mathrm{C-N/C=N}\) shifted from 285.40 to \(285.60~\mathrm{eV},\) while the peaks of \(\mathrm{C-C/C=C}\) and \(\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{O}\) remain unchanged. This result proved that \(\mathrm{C-N/C=N}\) interacts with uranium [58, 59]. After adsorption of uranium \((Z\mathrm{IF}{-}67@S i O_{2}{-}A/\mathrm{PAM{-}U})\) , a new peak was observed at ZIF-67@SiO2-A/PAM-U at \(908\ \ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}\) , corresponding to the vibration of the \(\scriptstyle\mathrm{O=U=O}\) bond (Fig. 6(f)), confirming the successful anchoring of U(VI) at ZIF-67@SiO2-A/PAM [60]. Figure 6(g) shows the EDS mappings diagram of uranium adsorbed by ZIF-67@SiO2-A/PAM, from which it can be clearly seen that uranium elements are uniformly distributed on ZIF\(67@S i O_{2}\) -A/PAM.
The mechanism of uranium adsorption by ZIF-67@SiO2- A/PAM was further analyzed by DFT. The binding mode of U(VI) on ZIF-67@SiO2-A/PAM is explored by using the first principles method of DFT. The binding model of ZIF-67@SiO2- A/PAM with uranyl ion was established and optimized, and the optimized structure is shown in Fig. 7. The calculation results reveal that the binding energies of PAM-U, \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) -A-U, and \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}.\) - A/PAM-U are \(-6.28,\) –45.03, and \(-67.46\ \mathrm{\eV},\) respectively, indicating that the chemisorption behavior between uranium and PAM, and \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) -A, and \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) -A/PAM occurs in the form of the coordination of uranium with N and O [61, 62]. Meanwhile, the binding energy of ZIF-67-U is \(0.08\;\mathrm{~eV},\) implying that ZIF-67 adsorbs uranyl ions through a half-open nanocage [63–65].
## 4 Conclusions
In this work, ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A was grafted to PAM by chemical crosslinking method, and a new kind of ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM adsorbent was prepared for uranium extraction from seawater. The ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM presents high uranium adsorption performance with adsorption quantity of \(173.48\ \ \mathrm{mg\cdot g^{-1}}\) \((C_{0}\,=\,99.73\,\mathrm{~mg{\cdot}L^{-1}},\,\mathrm{pH}\,=\,6.0)\), as well as good selectivity \((K_{\mathrm{d}}\,=\,53.466\;\mathrm{mL{\cdot}g^{-1}})\). It is worth noting that ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM has
excellent reusability, with an elution rate of \((82.45 \pm 2.76)\%\) after 10 adsorption–desorption cycles and acid-base resistance in aqueous solutions of different pH values. In addition, the adsorbent showed a high uranium extraction capacity of \(6.33~\mathrm{mg\cdot g^{-1}}\) in natural seawater. Finally, XPS analysis and DFT calculation show that the chemical adsorption mechanism dominates the adsorption behavior of uranium by ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM. Compared with traditional pure MOF and other adsorbents, ZIF-67@SiO\(_2\)-A/PAM has higher adsorption efficiency. It is an excellent material for uranium extraction from seawater.
